s `{batch, coalesced, changes, elements, relationships}`, the last two the post-state of what `changes` names as changed, both `null` past 500 entities. Every result and event is a copy built by the `wire*` functions — over a direct port pair nothing is cloned, and an uncopied result would hand out the replica's own property bags.
   The issue store: when the tail makes the replica `ready`, the service builds a `LiveIssues` over its working copy and puts its sweep in the sweep slot, reporting `progress sweep` from `done` 0 to `done === total` for every run, a re-sweep's included; `close`, a new `open` and a divergence drop it with the replica. From then on `stage`, `unstage`, `applyDelta` and a ready replica's tail go through it, so each revalidates what it may have moved before it answers; while opening, `adoptStaged` and the tail go to the working copy alone, and the first sweep covers them. `issues_version` is a per-service counter that never goes back: it moves by what the store's `version` moved (a splice that changed the store's content, an applied delta, the store become unusable), and by one when `setArtifacts`, `putArtifacts` or `setStagedArtifacts` flips whether a `validation_rules` artifact resolves (the 501 below comes or goes), which a ready replica posts at once as a bare `changed`. A transition's `changed` carries it; at a slice's end, if it moved since the last `changed` posted — sweep steps, or a probe that met an unusable pattern — the service posts a bare `changed` (the current `rev` and `staged_version`, no ids, `structural: false`), which neither restarts the digest check nor counts as a transition. Three methods answer from the store, in the routes' bodies (`bodies.ts`): `getModelIssues {}` (`issueListBody`) and `previewCommit {base_rev, batch_ids, strict}` (`previewBody`, the model half) are model-lane transitions that post no `changed` — they may probe, a rewind and replay, so they wait for a running scan and are never answered at its slice boundary, as a read would be; `validateModel {batch_ids}` is a transition that restarts the sweep, answered by a second one, queued once that sweep has ended, that builds `validateBody` — refused 409 `replica is not ready` if the replica was closed, replaced or diverged meanwhile, and 500 if the sweep threw. Each refuses first what the engine must not answer, with 501 for the client to take to the server: `reaches an unsupported pattern` (the store is `unusable`) and `reaches validation rules` (a `validation_rules` artifact resolves, a staged one included: `ArtifactSet.resolvesKind`); then, with 409, what the shell retries at the server: `stale base_rev` (`previewCommit`'s `base_rev` is not the replica's `rev`) and `stale staged batches` (`batch_ids` are not `staged()`'s ids, in order — checked again when `validateModel`'s answer is built). An answer whose probe ran anew (another `origins()` than the last one read, with batches staged) restarts the digest check, since a probe rebuilds the committed images it walks. A probe whose replay cannot even put the staged batches back diverges the replica, and the call is refused 409 `replica is not ready`. A direct call reads the store as it stands, partial until the first sweep has ended.
 - **`bench/run.ts`** (`pixi run engine-bench`, input written once by `pixi run engine-bench-data` → `scripts/snapshot_v2.py`) measures model M in three passes: opening the snapshot against opening the same model as one document in the same pass, the digest check, the heap of one open replica, a 1,000-op batch staged and unstaged, a rebase of 100 staged batches, a delta — and the long operations driven step by step by hand (`stepped`), each with its total and its longest step: the index build (over the document model), the digest check, the broadest search (`q='a'`, hit sort included), a rare one, a criteria scan (`property name contains 'a'` over every element) and a navigation (an untyped scope, one `Owns` hop from every element, the sort of every id included). Then the issue store over the same replica: its sweep (total, its first step — every id listed, O(model) in one step — and its longest step after it), the 1,000-op batch staged and unstaged through `LiveIssues`, the origin probe over 100 staged batches, and the delta over them, each revalidating; and a second sweep with 20,000 copies of one element staged, a single uniqueness group whose members each read the whole group (model M holds no issue). It measures STEPS, not slices: there is no scheduler in it; the browser benchmark below bounds the slices. `bench/parity-large.ts` (`pixi run engine-parity-large`) holds the sweep to the oracle at M over injected violations, since M itself holds none: `scripts/issues_large.py` derives from `benchmarks/large.model.json`, deterministically, one batch of ops (`benchmarks/large.violations.ops.json`, ~7,300 ops) that breaks every check an op can break on this metamodel — wrong datatypes and enum values, mistyped, non-string and dangling references (set to a ghost id, or left by deleting the element), property and end multiplicities, min / max / pattern / max_length on elements and relationships, endpoint types, second containment parents, containment cycles with elements below them, small duplicate groups (keyed by properties and by containment owner) and one of 3,001 members — lands it through the ops route's `_apply_batch`, runs the server's own sweep (`start_validation_sweep(sync=True)`) and writes `benchmarks/large.issues.json`, the sorted `issueKey`s (~7,700); the engine lands the same batch on the snapshot written from that document through `applyBatch`, sweeps it with a `LiveIssues`, and the two are compared as multisets — any difference fails the run, the first 20 printed, and both sides print their counts per check. An unknown type (no op creates one) and a key-relationship duplicate (the metamodel keys on no relationship) are the two it cannot reach. It weighs the heap BEFORE it reads the document: the last text a regular expression ran over stays reachable in V8, and there that is 77 MB.
   **`frontend/bench/`** (`pixi run engine-bench-browser`, which builds the sandbox first) is the same model in the browser: a bench page on `http://127.0.0.1:5173` (`bench/vite.config.ts`: the app's COOP / COEP plugin, and `benchmarks/large.snapshot.v2.gz` — the encoder's own bytes, which `engine-bench-data` writes with `--gzip` — served at `/data/snapshot.gz` with its length and no `Content-Encoding`) drives the BUILT sandbox through `frame.ts` and `client.ts`, and `bench/run.ts` (Node's type stripping) drives the page through `window.bench` with Playwright's default headless shell, e2e's browser: three passes, each in a fresh context, medians, a verdict against CN-3, never a failing exit. It measures the cold open (first byte asked to `replica ready`, split at the last chunk sent, `end`, and when `parse` and `index` began and ended by the engine's `progress` events), the digest check, `stage` / `unstage` / `applyDelta` and the reads THROUGH THE PORT (round trips, so upper bounds), and the longest slice FROM OUTSIDE: a loop posts `staged` — a `now` method, answered on the worker's next host turn — each when the last was answered, so its longest round trip bounds the longest time the worker took no message, main thread included. The heap is the WORKER's: Chromium runs with `--remote-debugging-port`, the `worker` target of `/json/list` whose `url` holds `engine-worker` is reached over its own `webSocketDebuggerUrl` with Node's `WebSocket`, `HeapProfiler.collectGarbage` then `Runtime.getHeapUsage`, after the digest check is done (before, it is 15–35 MB higher); `backingStorageSize` is shown beside `usedSize`, not added. A worker has no `performance.memory`, and `context.newCDPSession(frame)` needs an out-of-process frame the headless shell does not give. The page cannot compute a state digest, so the last row's delta carries a wrong one and ends the replica — after the rewind, apply and replay it times.
 - **`src/debug/`** — `dumpIndexes` (the canonical index dump the fixtures compare), `verifyConsistent` (incremental indexes against a rebuild, performed in place) and `shuffleAdjacency` (tests shuffle between steps to expose a dependence on adjacency order).
-- **Golden fixtures** — `tests/golden/scenarios/*.py` run the real Python core and `pixi run golden-fixtures` writes `engine/fixtures/golden/*.json`; `engine/test/**/*.golden.test.ts` read them. The same task writes the generated sources `driver.GENERATED` lists: `lower-tables.ts`, `digit-tables.ts` and `regex-tables.ts` under `engine/src/value/` (`tests/golden/lower_tables.py`, `coerce_tables.py`, `regex_tables.py`). `tests/golden/test_fixtures_current.py` (part of `core-test`) fails when a committed fixture or one of those sources differs from what the core produces now. The Python core is the oracle: fix the engine, never the fixture. Values a plain JSON reader cannot hold are tagged (`tests/golden/tagged.py` ↔ `engine/test/golden/load.ts`). Model scenarios are step lists run by `tests/golden/model_steps.py` (`Recorder`): every step records its result or error text, the state digest and a fingerprint of the entity lines plus `tests/golden/index_dump.py`'s dump, and every n-th step carries both in full; `engine/test/golden/model-steps.ts` replays them, a second time with every uniqueness key forced into one bucket. A `batch` step runs an op batch through `routes/ops.py::_apply_batch` on the recorder's own model, as a session does — a refused batch must leave no trace, the recorder fails the run when one does, and `ops_refused` refuses after every kind of touch — and records the outcome, the first-touch before-images, the inverse ops and the delta a replica would be sent; `undo` runs an earlier batch's inverse ops in restore mode. A `read` step calls the real route function behind a `READS` or `EVALUATIONS` name on a `Session` built for the call, with EVERY argument passed (called directly, a route keeps its `Query(...)` objects as defaults; `searchModel`'s body goes through `SearchQueryIn.model_validate`), and records `model_dump(mode="json")`; the replay looks the method up in `READS`, then in `EVALUATIONS` (drained, over `{model, artifacts, placements}`), and compares `JSON.stringify` of both, values and key order at once. `artifacts` replaces the project artifacts a step may fetch (the replay's `ArtifactSet`, committed — or, in `staged.test.ts`, staged as creates under their own ids over an empty committed layer); `evaluateNavigation`'s route runs over a stand-in database session that serves them. `navigate` runs the core's evaluator itself with the step's limits and row binding, value nodes tagged, and `has_script` `navigation_has_script`, both on a definition whose refs resolve against those artifacts. `view` registers the element ids a view's nested folders place (`read._placed_element_ids`, the list the shell would register) and `drop_view` forgets them. `validate` runs `ValidationPipeline(default_validators())` over a scope — a list of ids, or `all_ids`: every element id in state order, then every relationship id — and records the issues as `IssueOut` bodies, which the replay compares as text with `validateScoped`'s; after a `rules` step it runs `pipeline_for` of the recorder's compile, and the replay passes its own. `rules` takes `(artifact_id, name, yaml)` sources, sorted by name then id as both sides compile them, and records `routes/rules.py::parse_result` per source, the compile's `rules_status` and each compiled rule's applies types and paths with the checks per type; the replay hands the sources in as committed rule-set artifacts carrying those parses, lists them with `ruleSources` and compiles them. `reach` records `expand_scope` over its ids. A `batch` or `undo` with `record_dirty` adds its `DirtyCollector`'s ids to its result, which the replay compares with the ids its own collector gathered; a `batch` with `expand` too records them widened by `expand_scope`, as `expand_dirty` widens them. `seed` gives the recorder a `Session` over its model whose store the server's own sweep fills (`start_validation_sweep(sync=True)`); from then on every landed batch bumps `model_rev` and goes through `_finalize` as `POST /model/ops` does, and `issues` records `routes/validation.py::list_issues`, which the replay answers from an `IssueStore` it seeds and splices the same way (`storeListBody`). `preview` (with `strict`, the session's strict mode) and `validate_staged` send a step's ops to `routes/commits.py::preview_commit` and to the staged branch of `routes/validation.py::validate_model`, which apply, validate and roll back; the recorder holds both to leaving the model and the store as they were, and puts back any id a preview minted (creates carry `id` hints, so both sides name them alike). The replay clones the model into a `WorkingCopy`, gives it a `LiveIssues` seeded with a copy of its store, stages the ops as one batch and answers `previewBody` / `validateBody`. `insert_element` / `insert_relationship` put an entity in as committed state arrives (`Model.insert_element`), its type unchecked, so a scenario can hold a type its metamodel lacks. `engine/test/working/replica.golden.test.ts` feeds a `WorkingCopy` those deltas alone and expects the oracle's state, entity order included — through `ops_recreate` too (an entity created again under its id, unchanged in type and ends), which only the delta's `recreated_*` lists can express. `snapshot_v2` holds a snapshot text as the server encodes it, the texts no writer emits that the server reads alike (`same`) and the ones its decoder refuses in its own words (`refused`); `engine/test/snapshot/open.golden.test.ts` opens them all, cut into pieces as small as one byte. Every `observe()` of the golden runner computes its digest with the engine's own hash, so each fixture step holds it to `hashlib`.
